<!-- AREAS -->
<section style="padding:76px 24px 60px;background:#fff;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;text-align:center;">
    <span class="kicker">Service Areas</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,36px);margin:0 0 16px;">Serving Geelong and the Barwon region</h2>
    <div class="rich" style="text-align:left;"><p>We cover the whole Barwon region. That means <a href="/asbestos-removal-geelong/">Geelong</a> itself and the northern corridor through <a href="/asbestos-removal-lara/">Lara and Corio</a>, where the region's heaviest concentration of 1950s and 1960s asbestos cement housing sits, the Bellarine through <a href="/asbestos-removal-drysdale/">Drysdale and Clifton Springs</a> and <a href="/asbestos-removal-ocean-grove/">Ocean Grove</a>, the Surf Coast at <a href="/asbestos-removal-torquay/">Torquay</a>, and west to <a href="/asbestos-removal-colac/">Colac</a> and the Golden Plains, where older town housing and farm outbuildings throw up the same problem with far fewer local specialists.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EAF5FB;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How do I know whether it is actually asbestos?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">You cannot tell by looking. Asbestos cement was used in Australian houses from the mid 1940s until the late 1980s, and a house built or extended before 1990 may well contain it, but the sheet itself looks like ordinary fibre cement. Under Victoria's OHS Regulations, only a laboratory approved by NATA can identify asbestos in a sample, which is why a test is the honest first step. Analysis runs $88 to $160 per sample and most houses need three to eight samples.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How does pricing and scoping work?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Every quote comes from the licensed removalist after they have seen the material, not from us. Published Victorian rates give you a sense of the size of the job: a whole fibro fence $1,000 to $3,500, a house roof $4,000 to $15,000 or $40 to $120 per square metre, a single garage $2,500 to $5,000, a full set of eaves $1,500 to $2,500. Small jobs are governed by the minimum call out charge of $800 to $1,500, which is why a four sheet repair does not cost four times the $20 to $50 per sheet rate. Those are Melbourne metro benchmarks, since no Geelong pricing is published anywhere, and GST treatment varies between providers, so ask each removalist whether their quote includes GST.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How quickly can this happen?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Testing is the fast part. Standard laboratory turnaround is 1 to 5 business days from the day the sample arrives, and labs commonly add 25% to 50% for a result inside 24 to 48 hours. Removal takes longer to start than people expect, because a licensed removalist has to notify WorkSafe Victoria at least 24 hours before a job covering 10 square metres or less of non friable material, and at least five days before anything larger. If something has been broken and you are worried about it, call now and keep everyone away from the area in the meantime.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I take the fence down myself?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">The figure you will see quoted is 10 square metres of non friable material and no more than one hour of removal work in any seven day period. Both caps have to hold, and the hour is cumulative across jobs. Those regulations bind people doing this as work at a workplace, and WorkSafe's compliance code says they do not apply to someone removing asbestos at their own home, because a home is not a workplace. WorkSafe still recommends homeowners follow the same code, and the caps become law the moment a tradesperson, a tenant, a rental property or a paid helper is involved. A full fence line, a garage or a house roof is far past both caps, and friable material always needs a Class A licensed removalist.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Where is the asbestos allowed to go?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Only to a landfill authorised to receive it. EPA Victoria pre classifies asbestos waste as reportable priority waste, and not all landfills are authorised, so the rule is to ring ahead: an authorised site can still decline a load or require a booking. It cannot go in a general skip or a council hard waste collection. Sheets have to be wrapped in polythene around 200 microns thick, sealed with tape and marked with the asbestos warning label before they move. Gate fees at other Victorian councils run $100 to $376 per tonne, which gives you a sense of scale, though none of those is Geelong. There is more on <a href="/asbestos-disposal-geelong/">disposal in the Barwon region</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need a clearance certificate afterwards?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Not if the work was only non friable material not exceeding 10 square metres in total. Above that, Class A and Class B work needs a clearance certificate, and where the job is at a home and the owner engaged the removalist, the duty to obtain it sits on the licence holder rather than on you. It has to come from an independent person, who cannot be the removalist or anyone with a financial interest in the job, and published Victorian rates put a visual clearance certificate at $165 to $250. Air monitoring is a Class A requirement at $250 to $600 per day, not a routine part of a fence or roof job.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

